WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.334
Every December, 700 million people open Spotify Wrapped at almost the same moment.

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:10.669
Each one wants a year of their own listening, summarized and instant.

00:00:10.669 --> 00:00:15.003
Behind each card sit 365 billion play events.

00:00:15.003 --> 00:00:19.337
You cannot scan a year of plays the second someone taps.

00:00:19.337 --> 00:00:23.672
So how does the card come back in a blink.

00:00:23.672 --> 00:00:28.006
The hard work already happened, last night, while everyone slept.

00:00:28.006 --> 00:00:33.607
Watch the whole design in one picture.

00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:39.209
A year of raw plays lands chronologically, so your thousand listens scatter across a thousand pages.

00:00:39.209 --> 00:00:44.810
Fetching them, even with an index on user i-d, is a thousand random seeks, and doing that for everyone takes weeks.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:50.412
So overnight, before December, Spotify reorders the table to cluster all of one user's plays onto the same page.

00:00:50.412 --> 00:00:56.013
Your whole year now sits together, and a batch job reads each user in one sequential scan instead of a thousand seeks.

00:00:56.013 --> 00:01:01.614
From that scan it writes one tiny summary row per user, keyed on user i-d, into a small Wrapped table.

00:01:01.614 --> 00:01:07.216
On December first your card is a single hash index lookup on user i-d, constant time, which is what millions of simultaneous taps need.

00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:12.817
One catch lives in this layout.

00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:18.418
A table can be clustered one way only.

00:01:18.418 --> 00:01:24.020
Sorted by user i-d, Wrapped and your own history are fast, but the top fans of one artist are now scattered.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.621
So Spotify keeps multiple physical copies of Listens, each clustered for a different feature.

00:01:29.621 --> 00:01:35.223
Storage is cheap.

00:01:35.223 --> 00:01:40.824
Disk seeks at query time are not.

00:01:40.824 --> 00:01:47.117
Wrapped feels instant for one reason.

00:01:47.117 --> 00:01:53.409
The expensive aggregation ran overnight, and the online path is a tiny hash lookup on a table clustered by user i-d.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:59.701
Match the index to the dominant access pattern, and clustering turns a thousand random reads into one.

00:01:59.701 --> 00:02:05.994
The right physical layout, chosen the night before, is what lets 700 million cards come back the moment they are tapped.
